<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsam al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 963–998</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abu Kalijar Marzuban · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsam al-Dawla was a Buyid emir, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, who succeeded his father in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 983. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-dawla">Sharaf al-Dawla</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> from him in 987 and had him imprisoned and partly blinded. Released after Sharaf's death, he ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> from 989 until 998, when he was killed in a revolt led by sons of his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Samsam al-Dawla" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samsam_al-Dawla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Kalijar Marzuban was born in 963, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> and Sayyida bint Siyahgil, a daughter of the Gilite ruler Siyahgil, which made him a distant relation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarid dynasty</a>. In his father's lifetime he governed Buyid Oman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>. Although Shirdil was the eldest son, Marzuban was considered the heir, but Adud al-Dawla never settled the question. Marzuban was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> when his father died and kept the death secret to secure his succession; on announcing it he took the title Samsam al-Dawla, Cutting Sword of the Dynasty, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> recognised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The disputed succession broke up the Buyid lands. Shirdil invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-dawla">Sharaf al-Dawla</a>, two other brothers set up rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, and the Kurd Badh ibn Hasanwaih forced Samsam al-Dawla to confirm him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-bakr">Diyar Bakr</a>. In 983 the Turkic soldiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> went over to Sharaf, until Ziyar ibn Shahrakawayh, a maternal relative, persuaded most of them to stay. Samsam al-Dawla defeated the Dailamite rebel Saffar ibn Quddawiyah in 985 and took Basra and Khuzestan early in 986.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-dawla">Sharaf al-Dawla</a> then broke a peace between them, took Ahwaz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a>, and a revolt in Samsam's own army forced him to surrender. After Sharaf's death Samsam al-Dawla left prison and, aided by Sharaf's former vizier Ala ibn Hasan, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> from his brother Baha' al-Dawla. Threatened by their uncle Fakhr al-Dawla, the two brothers allied as equals, each taking the title of king, Samsam keeping Arrajan, Fars and Kerman. When Baha' al-Dawla attacked in 991, he was defeated, and Samsam al-Dawla regained Khuzestan and the Buyid lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>To strengthen his position Samsam al-Dawla recognised Fakhr al-Dawla as senior amir. Fakhr's death in 997, and growing trouble within Samsam's realm, left Baha' al-Dawla the strongest of the Buyid princes. With the support of the Kurdish ruler Badr ibn Hasanwaih he invaded in December 998. Fleeing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, Samsam al-Dawla was murdered near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> by one of the sons of '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a>. Baha' al-Dawla took Shiraz and reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>. Historians judge that Samsam al-Dawla lacked his father's qualities and never gained a firm grip on the affairs of his state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samsam al-Dawla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Adud al-Dawla's death in 983 his sons <b>Samsam al-Dawla</b>, Sharaf al-Dawla and Baha al-Dawla fought for the inheritance; Sharaf al-Dawla took Baghdad in 987, and Baha al-Dawla, ruling from 989 to 1012, reunited Iraq and Fars but could not restore Adud's authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
